      "math_academy_id": "q-51265",
      "sequence_position": 15,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190876/assets/6fe60368dc9dddb25380d76a34e8d3d7d2b2db4aa61e1d3333dda8242c37ccb0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from positive to negative) when we pass through $x=3$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=3$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190876/assets/9676ddaa53d496b0232940b54cbb618a901c2cdc730be0b203e3acc255732b77.png)",
